WEBVTT

00:09.000 --> 00:10.000
[Narrator] Imagine that you live next door

00:10.000 --> 00:12.000
to a single mother and a five-year-old son.

00:13.000 --> 00:15.000
Every morning you see her leave for work,

00:15.000 --> 00:18.000
but realise the child is being left at home by himself.

00:19.000 --> 00:21.000
How do you think the child feels

00:21.000 --> 00:23.000
being left all alone every day?

00:23.000 --> 00:24.000
What kind of danger is he in?

00:25.000 --> 00:28.000
Or imagine we suddenly see a pregnant woman

00:28.000 --> 00:29.000
running out of her house crying

00:29.000 --> 00:32.000
in the middle of the winter with no coat or even shoes.

00:34.000 --> 00:36.000
What do you think might be happening?

00:36.000 --> 00:37.000
And what can you do?

00:39.000 --> 00:41.000
Or how would you react if you were a council worker

00:41.000 --> 00:44.000
on a visit to support a housing tenant,

00:44.000 --> 00:46.000
who discovered a disturbing scene of self-neglect

00:46.000 --> 00:49.000
and clear signs of mental illness?

00:49.000 --> 00:52.000
All throughout Haringey, there are people in trouble.

00:52.000 --> 00:54.000
Children and adults who are vulnerable

00:54.000 --> 00:57.000
and living in conditions or experiencing situations

00:57.000 --> 01:00.000
where they are in danger of harm and abuse.

01:00.000 --> 01:03.000
But you can help to make them safer.

01:03.000 --> 01:04.000
These films will show you how.

01:05.000 --> 01:08.000
All of these stories and people are real,

01:08.000 --> 01:12.000
and so are the opportunities for you to make a difference.

01:17.000 --> 01:19.000
So what do we mean by safeguarding?

01:19.000 --> 01:22.000
Safeguarding is protecting children and adults at risk

01:22.000 --> 01:24.000
from harm and abuse.

01:24.000 --> 01:26.000
It's about promoting their welfare.

01:26.000 --> 01:29.000
People don't always know they're being abused,

01:29.000 --> 01:32.000
so we all have the opportunity to help and protect them.

01:32.000 --> 01:35.000
How do we safeguard children and adults?

01:35.000 --> 01:37.000
By paying attention to signs of abuse

01:37.000 --> 01:38.000
and telling someone about it.

01:39.000 --> 01:42.000
Watch this true story about safeguarding.

01:42.000 --> 01:44.000
Think about how you would react in this situation

01:44.000 --> 01:46.000
and what you could do to make a difference.

01:48.000 --> 01:49.000
[Woman] I went out on a home visit one day,

01:49.000 --> 01:51.000
myself and a colleague, to talk to the tenant

01:51.000 --> 01:55.000
about rent arrears and their benefits,

01:55.000 --> 01:57.000
and on getting there, knocked on the door.

01:57.000 --> 02:00.000
Child answered the door from the inside,

02:00.000 --> 02:02.000
didn't open the door and asked who it was.

02:02.000 --> 02:04.000
I said I was from the council and needed

02:04.000 --> 02:05.000
to speak to the mother.

02:05.000 --> 02:08.000
He said, "Well, my mum's not here."

02:08.000 --> 02:12.000
And I instantly realised, okay,

02:12.000 --> 02:13.000
this child I was talking to

02:13.000 --> 02:16.000
couldn't have been more than five, six years old,

02:16.000 --> 02:19.000
chatting with us very freely through the letter box.

02:19.000 --> 02:21.000
At first he said she'd popped out to the shops,

02:21.000 --> 02:25.000
and then we said, "Okay, we'll wait until she came back."

02:25.000 --> 02:27.000
Half an hour later, she still wasn't back.

02:27.000 --> 02:30.000
At this point we started to get a bit concerned.

02:30.000 --> 02:32.000
When I realised that the child was on their own

02:32.000 --> 02:34.000
I was anxious, worried.

02:34.000 --> 02:36.000
The neighbour confirmed that the parent,

02:36.000 --> 02:37.000
they were a single mother,

02:37.000 --> 02:39.000
often left the child to go to work,

02:39.000 --> 02:41.000
so she would leave the child on their own.

02:41.000 --> 02:43.000
I had to contact my manager,

02:43.000 --> 02:46.000
who then contacted social services,

02:46.000 --> 02:49.000
and well, yes, social services came in

02:49.000 --> 02:51.000
and took over the situation.

02:51.000 --> 02:53.000
It could have happened at a different occasion

02:53.000 --> 02:55.000
where the child could have come to harm,

02:55.000 --> 02:57.000
'cause anything could happen.

02:57.000 --> 02:58.000
That is neglect.

03:01.000 --> 03:03.000
[Narrator] If you see a situation where a person or child

03:03.000 --> 03:05.000
is in danger of harm or abuse,

03:05.000 --> 03:07.000
acting quickly can have a major impact.

03:08.000 --> 03:11.000
In our story of the child being left at home alone,

03:11.000 --> 03:13.000
imagine what would happen if the council worker

03:13.000 --> 03:14.000
hadn't reported it.

03:15.000 --> 03:18.000
Something bad could have happened to that child,

03:18.000 --> 03:20.000
or the mother who is struggling to cope

03:20.000 --> 03:22.000
might fall into more trouble.

03:22.000 --> 03:24.000
But the council worker did report it.

03:24.000 --> 03:27.000
That means a child can be kept safe

03:27.000 --> 03:29.000
and the mother can get the support she needs.

03:30.000 --> 03:32.000
Had someone noticed and reported this even earlier,

03:33.000 --> 03:34.000
for example the neighbours,

03:34.000 --> 03:35.000
they may have gotten the support

03:35.000 --> 03:37.000
they needed even sooner.

03:38.000 --> 03:40.000
Think about this story about safeguarding.

03:40.000 --> 03:42.000
[Man] Mr Smith is an elderly gentleman,

03:42.000 --> 03:45.000
he attends the local post office every Monday,

03:45.000 --> 03:48.000
uses his cashpoint card, takes out 20 pounds

03:49.000 --> 03:50.000
and buys his groceries.

03:50.000 --> 03:53.000
He knows all the people on the counters,

03:53.000 --> 03:56.000
he tells them about their day, what he's about to do.

03:56.000 --> 03:58.000
On one occasion, Mr Smith doesn't come in,

03:58.000 --> 04:01.000
but another gentleman comes in with his card

04:01.000 --> 04:03.000
and has the PIN, so he's using the PIN.

04:03.000 --> 04:05.000
The post office staff query this,

04:05.000 --> 04:07.000
and he says he's a relative of Mr Smith.

04:07.000 --> 04:10.000
Ordinarily Mr Smith uses 20 pounds to do his shopping.

04:10.000 --> 04:12.000
This gentleman has taken out 50.

04:12.000 --> 04:13.000
There's concerns.

04:13.000 --> 04:16.000
The post office staff, they contact the safeguarding team

04:16.000 --> 04:18.000
because of the concerns they have.

04:18.000 --> 04:21.000
We look into it and it transpires that this gentleman,

04:21.000 --> 04:24.000
he's claiming to be a carer for Mr Smith.

04:24.000 --> 04:26.000
Once the local authority started their inquiries,

04:26.000 --> 04:27.000
this gentleman disappeared.

04:27.000 --> 04:29.000
The local authority put in steps

04:29.000 --> 04:31.000
to ensure that they would safeguard his finances.

04:31.000 --> 04:34.000
The local authority gives Mr Smith his weekly money,

04:34.000 --> 04:36.000
so he's able to go and do his shopping as he used to do.

04:38.000 --> 04:40.000
[Narrator] If you live or work in the borough,

04:40.000 --> 04:42.000
you have an opportunity to help protect

04:42.000 --> 04:44.000
our most vulnerable people

04:44.000 --> 04:46.000
simply by keeping your eyes open,

04:46.000 --> 04:50.000
using your instincts and telling someone about it.

04:50.000 --> 04:52.000
In all of these films, you'll see more true stories

04:52.000 --> 04:55.000
that show the different forms that abuse can take,

04:55.000 --> 04:58.000
what to look out for,

04:58.000 --> 05:00.000
and just how much of a difference it can make

05:00.000 --> 05:02.000
to someone's life.
